#v(-2em)
#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.5pt)
#v(2em)

/ CNCF: Cloud Native Computing Foundation. A vendor-neutral open-source foundation that hosts and sustains cloud-native projects including Kubernetes, Istio, and Linkerd, under the Linux Foundation umbrella.
/ CNI: Container Network Interface. A specification and set of libraries for configuring network interfaces in Linux containers. In Kubernetes, CNI plugins (such as Flannel) manage pod networking, including IP address assignment, routing, and the iptables rules that redirect traffic through sidecar proxies.
/ CORS: Cross-Origin Resource Sharing. An HTTP mechanism that allows or restricts web applications running at one origin from making requests to a different origin, controlled through HTTP headers such as `Origin` and `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`.
/ eBPF: Extended Berkeley Packet Filter. A Linux kernel technology that allows sandboxed programs to run in kernel space without modifying kernel source code, used by tools such as Cilium for high-performance network security enforcement.
/ Envoy: An open-source edge and service proxy originally developed at Lyft and now maintained by the Cloud Native Computing Foundation (CNCF). Istio uses Envoy as its data plane sidecar proxy.
/ gRPC: A high-performance, open-source Remote Procedure Call (RPC) framework developed by Google. It uses HTTP/2 as its transport protocol and Protocol Buffers as its interface definition language.
/ iptables: A Linux kernel facility for configuring IPv4 packet filtering and network address translation rules. In Kubernetes sidecar deployments, iptables rules transparently redirect inbound and outbound pod traffic through the sidecar proxy without requiring changes to application code.
/ ISO: The International Organization for Standardization is an international standard development organization composed of representatives from the national standards organizations of member countries.
/ Istio: An open-source service mesh platform that uses Envoy as its data plane sidecar proxy and Istiod as its control plane. It provides L7 traffic management, mTLS, and authorization policy enforcement via a push-based xDS configuration model.
/ JWT: JSON Web Token. An open standard (RFC 7519) for securely transmitting claims between parties as a digitally signed JSON object. In service mesh contexts, JWTs carry identity assertions that proxies can validate cryptographically, for example using RS256 signatures.
/ Kubernetes (K8s): An open-source container orchestration platform that automates the deployment, scaling, and lifecycle management of containerized workloads. It is the standard runtime environment for service mesh deployments and provides the networking and scheduling infrastructure on which this study runs.
/ L4: Layer 4 of the OSI model, the Transport Layer. Security at this layer operates on TCP/UDP connections, IP addresses, and ports without inspecting application-layer content.
/ L7: Layer 7 of the OSI model, the Application Layer. Security at this layer can inspect and act on HTTP headers, methods, paths, gRPC method names, and other application-level attributes.
/ Linkerd: An open-source service mesh built on a Rust-based sidecar proxy (linkerd2-proxy). It prioritizes low latency and minimal resource overhead, using a pull-based just-in-time service discovery model and providing mTLS by default.
/ mTLS: Mutual Transport Layer Security. An extension of TLS in which both the client and server authenticate each other using certificates, providing cryptographic workload identity in service mesh environments.
/ NIST: NIST is the National Institute of Standards and Technology at the U.S. Department of Commerce.
/ p95 / p99: Latency percentile metrics. p95 is the latency value below which 95% of requests complete, representing the experience of the slowest 5% of users. p99 captures the slowest 1%. These high-percentile metrics are used as the primary latency indicator throughout this study because they expose tail behaviour that averages conceal.
/ PDP: Policy Decision Point. In the NIST SP 800-207 Zero Trust model, the PDP is the combination of the Policy Engine and Policy Administrator that evaluates access requests and issues authorization decisions. In a service mesh, the control plane acts as the PDP.
/ PEP: Policy Enforcement Point. In the NIST SP 800-207 Zero Trust model, the PEP is the component that enforces access decisions by allowing or denying traffic. In a service mesh, the sidecar proxy acts as the PEP.
/ PKI: Public Key Infrastructure. A framework of policies, procedures, and technologies for managing digital certificates and public-key encryption. In service meshes, PKI is used to issue and rotate workload identity certificates that underpin mTLS authentication.
/ RBAC: Role-Based Access Control. An access control model in which permissions are assigned to roles rather than individuals. In Kubernetes, RBAC governs which principals can perform operations on cluster resources, and misconfigured RBAC can expose sensitive service account tokens to attackers.
/ RESP: Redis Serialization Protocol. The wire protocol used by the Redis in-memory data store for client-server communication. Because RESP uses framing that service mesh proxies cannot parse as HTTP, both Istio and Linkerd treat Redis traffic as opaque TCP and bypass L7 inspection entirely.
/ RPS: Requests Per Second. A throughput metric measuring the rate at which a system processes requests. Used throughout this study as the primary measure of service capacity alongside p95 latency.
/ Service Mesh: A dedicated infrastructure layer for managing service-to-service communication in a microservices architecture. It is typically implemented via sidecar proxies that intercept all network traffic, providing mTLS encryption, L7 policy enforcement, and observability without changes to application code.
/ Sidecar: A design pattern in which a proxy container is deployed in the same Kubernetes Pod as an application container, transparently intercepting all inbound and outbound network traffic to provide security, observability, and traffic management capabilities.
/ TOCTOU: Time-of-Check to Time-of-Use. A class of software vulnerability in which the state of a resource changes between the point at which a security check is performed and the point at which the resource is used, potentially allowing an attacker to exploit the gap.
/ xDS: Discovery Service. A family of Envoy data plane APIs used by Istio's control plane (Istiod) to push configuration, policy, and routing updates to sidecar proxies via a push-based subscription model.
/ Zero Trust Architecture (ZTA): A security model based on the principle of "never trust, always verify", in which no user, device, or workload is implicitly trusted regardless of its network location. Every access request must be authenticated, authorised, and continuously validated.
